is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
The outage is caused by the BGP control-plane on router1, not by router2. From pc1, pings to 200.1.1.2 return “Destination Net Unreachable” sourced by 195.11.14.1 (router1), which indicates router1 has no route to 200.1.1.0/24. On router1, the routing table only contains connected routes (193.10.11.0/24 and 195.11.14.0/24), confirming it is not learning 200.1.1.0/24 via BGP. Direct inspection shows the FRR service on router1 is failed and bgpd is not running; “show bgp summary” returns “bgpd is not running.” Conversely, router2’s FRR is up, advertising 200.1.1.0/24, and its BGP neighbor to 193.10.11.1 is stuck in Active (waiting) because router1 never establishes the session.

Affected component: FRRouting (bgpd) on router1. Mechanism: with BGP down on router1, no EBGP adjacency forms over 193.10.11.0/24, so router1 lacks a path to 200.1.1.0/24 and generates ICMP net-unreachable to pc1. Localization: router1, BGP daemon/service. Root cause: the FRR service on router1 has stopped/failed, leaving bgpd down.

Corrective fix: restart FRR on router1 (systemctl restart frr) and ensure router1 has the correct BGP config (AS 1, neighbor 193.10.11.2 remote-as 2, and advertise 195.11.14.0/24). Do not restart router2. After restoring bgpd on router1, verify the BGP session establishes and that 200.1.1.0/24 appears in router1’s routing table, restoring pc1↔pc2 connectivity.
=== END ===